; DOMAIN: psychology/motivation-autonomy [CLOSED, derived 2026-07-26]
; Claim: Self-Determination Theory (Deci & Ryan) grounds intrinsic motivation in three basic psychological needs whose satisfaction predicts flourishing and whose thwarting predicts amotivation. (1) Three needs: autonomy (self-endorsed action), competence (effectance/mastery), relatedness (belonging). (2) Motivation lies on a continuum from amotivation through extrinsic (external, introjected, identified, integrated regulation) to intrinsic, ordered by degree of internalization/perceived autonomy. (3) Need satisfaction => intrinsic motivation, well-being, persistence; need frustration => controlled motivation, ill-being. (4) Autonomy-supportive vs controlling contexts shift regulation along the internalization continuum.
; Structure: (three needs: autonomy, competence, relatedness) (motivation continuum by internalization: amotivation->external->introjected->identified->integrated->intrinsic) (need satisfaction=>intrinsic+well-being; frustration=>controlled+ill-being) (autonomy-supportive contexts drive internalization).
; Result: Motivation quality, not just quantity, is set by how well a context satisfies autonomy/competence/relatedness; intrinsic motivation is the endpoint of internalization when the three needs are met.
; Bridge: genesis_free_energy_principle (competence = expected reduction of prediction error/effectance; autonomy = self-generated policy selection; need satisfaction lowers long-run free energy of the agent-context coupling) + genesis_neuroscience (intrinsic motivation and reward-prediction/dopaminergic effectance signals are the neural substrate; need frustration maps to stress/threat circuitry).